  "response_text": "Setting up a thread\u2019s stack means preparing memory so the CPU knows where to start executing the thread. We simulate a saved context by pushing fake register values (like xPSR, PC, and others) onto the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking the thread was already running, so it can \u201cresume\u201d it properly. Storing registers on the stack is essential to restore execution state during context switches.\\n",
